@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# InteriorGS semantic bridge
+
+This adapter converts one InteriorGS instance bbox into conservative per-Gaussian labels without changing FieryGS core code.
+
+## Existing interfaces
+
+- Camera JSON uses InteriorGS original world coordinates: `X=right`, `Y=back`, `Z=up`, in metres.
+- Cameras are OpenCV look-at cameras: camera axes are right, down, forward. Each record stores `width`, `height`, `fx`, `fy`, `cx`, `cy`, `position`, `target`, and `up`.
+- RGB and depth use FieryGS `gaussian_renderer.render_with_depth` and the unmodified `GaussianModel`.
+- Gaussian order is the vertex row order in `3dgs_explicit.ply`. No sorting or PLY rewriting occurs.
+- The ID pass uses PyTorch3D point rasterization with native FieryGS projected radii. `gaussian_id.png` stores `Gaussian ID + 1` losslessly in RGB24 little-endian channels; black is background.
+- `depth.png` is native rendered depth in millimetres as unsigned 16-bit PNG.
+
+## First experiment
+
+The first target is InteriorGS `instance_id=113`, category `table`. The default camera set contains 24 orbit views over three camera heights. A projected mask is the convex hull of all eight projected 3D bbox corners, never just the bbox centre.
+
+Voting counts a Gaussian at most once per view. A Gaussian is assigned when it is visible in at least three views, its `inside / visible` ratio is at least 0.7, and its centre lies inside the source oriented 3D bbox. The bbox is only a spatial candidate gate; the multi-view score still decides membership. A 3 cm floor clearance suppresses floor Gaussians at the bottom face. If another InteriorGS instance bbox overlaps the target, points inside that competing instance are conservatively removed; this resolves chair seats and decorations intruding into the table box without introducing a classifier.
+
+`gaussian_instance_labels.npy` follows the bridge diagnostic format: instance `113` for the table and `-1` for unassigned points. Exported `points_label_mulscale.npy` has one entry per PLY Gaussian and no negative labels: the table is FieryGS part `0`, while the rest of the scene is conservative background part `1`. Only part `0` is sent to the VLM; part `1` must remain unburnable during material mapping. This satisfies FieryGS's whole-array label invariant without pretending that the rest of the office has been semantically processed.
+
+## Commands
+
+Run from the FieryGS repository in the `fire_gaussian_win` environment:
+
+```powershell
+python -m adapter.semantic_bridge.generate_views --labels D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\labels.json --instance-id 113 --output D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\cameras.json
+python -m adapter.semantic_bridge.render_gaussian_maps --ply D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\3dgs_explicit.ply --cameras D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\cameras.json --views-dir D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\views
+python -m adapter.semantic_bridge.project_bbox --labels D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\labels.json --instance-id 113 --cameras D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\cameras.json --views-dir D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\views
+python -m adapter.semantic_bridge.gaussian_vote --views-dir D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\views --gaussian-count 927067 --ply D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\3dgs_explicit.ply --labels D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\labels.json --output D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\gaussian_instance_labels.npy
+python -m adapter.semantic_bridge.export_fierygs_labels --input D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\gaussian_instance_labels.npy --output D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\points_label_mulscale.npy
+python -m adapter.semantic_bridge.render_assignment_preview --ply D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\3dgs_explicit.ply --labels D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\gaussian_instance_labels.npy --cameras D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\cameras.json --output D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\table_assignment_preview.png
+python -m adapter.semantic_bridge.prepare_vlm_input --ply D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\3dgs_explicit.ply --labels D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\points_label_mulscale.npy --cameras D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\cameras.json --views-dir D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\views --output-dir D:\interiorgs_data\office_01\semantic_bridge\gpt_input
+```
+
+The final command produces `gpt_input/gpt_input_single/000.png`, the exact three-panel layout consumed by `vlm_predict.py`. The preview must be inspected before expanding to chairs or other categories. This bridge does not run fire simulation.
+
+For a native material run, place the label array and `gpt_input` folders in one model directory, run the unmodified `vlm_predict.py --vlm gpttool`, then invoke `map_native_materials.py`. The latter calls FieryGS's own parsing and Gaussian mapping functions; it only adds part `1` as an explicitly unprocessed, unburnable background so the native all-points assertion remains valid.
+
+`extract_material_voxels.py` then calls FieryGS's original `extract_voxel.create_voxel_grid` and `map_points_to_voxels_materials` functions. Combustible selection comes exclusively from `points_burnability.npy`; the configured simulation domain only defines grid extent and does not mark fuel.
